<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Wu (Ten Kingdoms)</h1><p class="st-meta">907 CE – 937 CE · capital Yangzhou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first of the southern Ten Kingdoms, ruled from Yangzhou by the Yang family and their powerful ministers, which became the basis of Southern Tang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-xingmi">Yang Xingmi</a>, a soldier of humble origins, won control of the Huainan region between the Huai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> rivers in the 890s and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> his base. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> named him Prince of Wu in 902, and after the dynasty fell in 907 his family refused to recognise the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wu was rich from salt production and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> trade, and fought repeated wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wuyue">Wuyue</a> and the northern dynasties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-xingmi">Yang Xingmi's</a> sons were weak, and real power passed to the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-wen">Xu Wen</a> and his adopted son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bian">Xu Zhigao</a>, who ran the government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Jinling</a> (Nanjing). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-pu">Yang Pu</a> took the title of emperor in 927.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bian">Xu Zhigao</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-pu">Yang Pu</a> abdicate and founded his own dynasty, soon renamed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> and known as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-tang">Southern Tang</a>, taking the name Li Bian. The Yang family was kept in confinement and later massacred.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wu-ten-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Wu (Ten Kingdoms)" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>907 CE – 937 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yangzhou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yang Xingmi, Yang Pu, Xu Wen (regent)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yang Wu, Huainan, Southern Wu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=922&amp;focus=wu-ten-kingdoms" aria-label="Wu (Ten Kingdoms) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7748 1000 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wu (Ten Kingdoms) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8068 1256L8056 1267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8068 1256h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8068 1256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yangzhou. <a href="/atlas?year=922&amp;focus=wu-ten-kingdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
